\title[The Pell Spine]{The Pell Spine: Strong Divisibility, Ranks of Apparition,\\ and a Cluster of Falsifications}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{1 September 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We develop the arithmetic of the \emph{Pell spine}, the pair of integer sequences $(Q_n,P_n)$ determined by $(1+\sqrt2)^n = Q_n + P_n\sqrt2$, under a deliberately adversarial methodology: every conjecture is stated so sharply that a single explicit counterexample can refute it. Seven such conjectures fall, all within the first thirty terms, and each refutation is converted into a sharper theorem.

The surviving results form a complete divisibility theory. The Pell numbers are a strong divisibility sequence, $\gcd(P_m,P_n) = P_{\gcd(m,n)}$, whence $m \mid n \iff P_m \mid P_n$. An apparition theorem holds: every modulus $m \ge 1$ divides some positive Pell number, by pigeonhole on the reversible state map $n \mapsto (P_n,P_{n+1}) \bmod m$. Combining the two yields the exact divisibility law $m \mid P_n \iff \rank(m) \mid n$, where $\rank(m)$ is the rank of apparition, together with $\rank(ab) = \lcmop(\rank(a),\rank(b))$ for coprime $a,b$. We prove a Fermat law $p \mid P_{p-1}P_{p+1}$ for every odd prime $p$, via a Frobenius expansion inside $\Z[\sqrt2]$ that produces Euler's criterion $P_p \equiv 2^{(p-1)/2} \pmod p$ along the way. We classify the solutions of $x^2-2y^2 = \pm1$ as exactly the spine points, classify the near-isosceles Pythagorean triples as exactly the odd-index spine points, and establish the exact approximation identity $|Q_n - P_n\sqrt2| = (\sqrt2-1)^n$.

For the companion strand we prove a parity-graded law: for $m \ge 2$, $Q_m \mid Q_n$ if and only if $n = mk$ with $k$ odd; and $\gcd(Q_m,Q_n)$ equals $Q_{\gcd(m,n)}$ when both index quotients are odd, and equals $1$ otherwise.

Among the falsifications, the most striking is the Wall--Sun--Sun analogue: the growth law $\rank(p^2)= p\,\rank(p)$ fails at $p = 13$, because $P_7 = 169 = 13^2$, and again at $p = 31$, because $31^2 \mid P_{30}$. Thus the Pell spine exhibits two Wall--Sun--Sun-type primes below $50$, whereas no Fibonacci Wall--Sun--Sun prime is known below $10^{17}$.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Pell numbers, silver ratio, strong divisibility sequence, rank of apparition, Wall--Sun--Sun prime, Pell equation, near-isosceles Pythagorean triples}

